import
	../../html

intro-overloading html[]()
	- section
		- p quote
			If multiple functions have the same name, they are called {em "overloaded"}.
			{br}
			They are still just ordinary functions with no special relationship other than having a name in common.
			{br}
			When Keen sees a function call, it creates a list of all functions in scope with that name.
			{br}
			It then filters them by:
		- bullets
			- "The number of arguments"
			- "The return type"
			- "Argument types"

		- p "After that, if there are multiple functions remaining, it is an error due to ambiguity."

	- section "Number of arguments",
		- p "Overloading by number of arguments is simple:"

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log foo 1
				log foo 1, 2

			foo nat(a nat)
				a * 10
			foo nat(a nat, b nat)
				a + b

		- aside-small "If you hover your mouse over a function call, the tooltip will tell you which function it calls."

	- section "Return type",
		- p "Overloading by return type uses the expected type of the expression."

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				n nat = foo
				s string = foo
				# This would be an error due to ambiguity
				# x = foo
				log n, s

			foo nat()
				1
			foo string()
				"a"

		- aside-small quote
			While local types are optional in general, they are needed above to provide an expected type to {fn-foo}.

		- p quote
			The expected type could also come from the argument type of an outer function.
			{br}
			Below, since {fn-bar} takes a {string}, that overload of {fn-foo} is used.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log bar foo

			bar string(a string)
				"foo \{a\}"
			foo nat() # not used
				1
			foo string()
				"a"

	- section "Argument type",
		- p "Basic overloading by argument type:"

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log foo 1
				log foo "a"

			foo nat(a nat)
				a * 10
			foo string(a string)
				a

	- section "Troubleshooting",
		- p quote
			If you are having trouble with a piece of code and want to be sure you are calling the intended overload,
			add type annotations on everything, as in:
			{br}
			{fn-foo}{colon-colon}{string}{sp}{fn-bar}{colon-colon}{symbol}
			{br}
			That way it's clear you want to call {fn-foo}{sp}{string}{lparen}{code "_"}{sp}{symbol}{rparen} .
			{br}
			Once the code is working, remove unnecessary type annotations.

		- p "More details of Keen type-checking are in {intro-link more-overloading}."
